      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Zero-Trust Architecture: AWS IAM OIDC Federation vs Long-Lived Static Keys
      </h2>
      <p>
        The most dangerous antipattern in CI/CD compliance automation is storing long-lived AWS IAM access keys (<code>AKIA...</code>) in GitHub Secrets. If those keys are accidentally leaked or over-permissioned, your compliance pipeline itself creates a critical security finding under Common Criteria CC6.1 and CC6.2.
      </p>
      <p>
        To eliminate static credentials completely, implement OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity federation between GitHub Actions and AWS IAM. With OIDC, each workflow run requests an ephemeral, cryptographically signed JSON Web Token (JWT) from GitHub's token authority. AWS STS verifies the token against your AWS IAM OIDC Identity Provider and issues temporary credentials that expire automatically after 15 to 60 minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trust policy below strictly binds credential assumption to your specific GitHub organization and audit repository:
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud": "sts.amazonaws.com"
        },
        "StringLike": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:my-organization/soc2-compliance-vault:ref:refs/heads/main"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Production GitHub Actions Workflow: Multi-Service Automated Evidence Extractor
      </h2>
      <p>
        The production workflow below is scheduled to execute every Monday at 04:00 UTC. It authenticates through OIDC, gathers system configurations across IAM, CloudTrail, KMS, and GitHub Branch Protections, creates a cryptographic SHA-256 manifest, and syncs the entire package into an immutable S3 bucket.
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>name: Continuous SOC 2 Evidence Collector

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 4 * * 1' # Runs every Monday at 04:00 UTC
  workflow_dispatch:     # Enables manual triggering for auditor spot checks

permissions:
  id-token: write      # Required for requesting the OIDC JWT token
  contents: read       # Read repository source tree

jobs:
  collect-evidence:
    name: Extract and Lock Audit Evidence
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 20

    steps:
      - name: Checkout Audit Repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Authenticate to AWS via OIDC Federation
        uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
        with:
          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/SOC2EvidenceCollectorRole
          role-session-name: GitHubActions-SOC2EvidenceExtraction
          aws-region: us-east-1

      - name: Initialize Evidence Workspace
        run: |
          mkdir -p evidence-bundle
          TIMESTAMP=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H%M%SZ)
          echo "TIMESTAMP=$TIMESTAMP" >> &#36;GITHUB_ENV
          echo "Evidence collection run initiated at $TIMESTAMP"

      - name: [CC6.1] Extract IAM Credential Report
        run: |
          echo "Generating IAM credential report..."
          aws iam generate-credential-report
          # Poll until report is ready
          for i in {1..12}; do
            STATUS=$(aws iam get-credential-report --query 'State' --output text 2>/dev/null || echo "PENDING")
            if [ "$STATUS" = "COMPLETE" ]; then
              echo "Report generation complete."
              break
            fi
            echo "Waiting for report generation (attempt $i)..."
            sleep 5
          done
          aws iam get-credential-report --output text --query 'Content' | base64 -d > evidence-bundle/iam-credential-report.csv

      - name: [CC6.3] Extract Account Authorization Details
        run: |
          aws iam get-account-authorization-details > evidence-bundle/iam-authorization-details.json

      - name: [CC7.2] Extract CloudTrail Active Configuration
        run: |
          aws cloudtrail describe-trails > evidence-bundle/cloudtrail-trails.json
          aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name OrganizationAuditTrail > evidence-bundle/cloudtrail-status.json

      - name: [CC8.1] Extract GitHub Branch Protection Configuration
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: &#36;{{ secrets.AUDIT_RO_TOKEN }}
        run: |
          gh api repos/my-org/core-saas-platform/branches/main/protection > evidence-bundle/branch-protection-main.json
          gh api repos/my-org/core-saas-platform/pulls?state=closed\&per_page=20 > evidence-bundle/recent-pr-reviews.json

      - name: Generate Cryptographic SHA-256 Checksum Manifest
        run: |
          cd evidence-bundle
          sha256sum * > SHA256SUMS.txt
          echo "--- Cryptographic Checksum Manifest ---"
          cat SHA256SUMS.txt

      - name: Upload Evidence to Immutable S3 Object Lock Vault
        run: |
          RETAIN_DATE=$(date -u -d "+365 days" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
          aws s3 sync evidence-bundle/ s3://company-soc2-immutable-audit-vault/weekly-bundles/&#36;{{ env.TIMESTAMP }}/ \
            --object-lock-mode COMPLIANCE \
            --object-lock-retain-until-date "$RETAIN_DATE"
          echo "Evidence successfully locked until $RETAIN_DATE"</code></pre>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Cryptographic Immutability: AWS S3 Object Lock in Compliance Mode &amp; SHA-256 Manifests
      </h2>
      <p>
        The primary reason SOC 2 auditors historically demanded manual screenshots was the fear of post-facto evidence tampering. If an engineering team stores audit evidence in a standard Google Drive or shared Dropbox folder, anyone with administrative access could theoretically modify logs before presenting them to the CPA.
      </p>
      <p>
        By provisioning an Amazon S3 bucket with <strong>Object Lock enabled in COMPLIANCE mode</strong>, you establish a Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) storage model governed by AWS physical infrastructure rules. In COMPLIANCE mode:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>No IAM principal, including the AWS account root user, can overwrite, alter, or delete any locked object during the retention period.</li>
        <li>The retention period cannot be shortened or disabled by any API call or support ticket.</li>
        <li>Every file uploaded receives a permanent version ID and an ETag hash verifying content integrity.</li>
      </ul>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code># Terraform Infrastructure-as-Code for Immutable Audit Vault
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "soc2_vault" {
  bucket        = "company-soc2-immutable-audit-vault"
  force_destroy = false

  object_lock_enabled = true

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "vault_versioning" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.soc2_vault.id
  versioning_configuration {
    status = "Enabled"
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "vault_crypto" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.soc2_vault.id
  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_object_lock_configuration" "vault_lock" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.soc2_vault.id

  rule {
    default_retention {
      mode  = "COMPLIANCE"
      years = 1
    }
  }
}</code></pre>

